#: ../../autodoc.rst:1
msgid "Documenting Python with autodoc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:4
msgid "Guidedog documents Python by reading its source. It does not import a package. Docstrings and signatures become part of the document graph. Objects created only at runtime need an explicit description. This is a deliberate boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:8
msgid "Setting it up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:11
msgid "We list the extension and tell Guidedog where our packages are, relative to the source folder, as ``sys.path`` tells Python:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:19
msgid "``guidedog migrate`` writes ``autodoc_source_paths`` for us. It reads the lines that ``conf.py`` files use to reach their package, such as ``sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath('..'))``. A package installed from elsewhere is found only if we add the folder that holds it, for example a source checkout of a dependency. Guidedog never looks in Python's own ``site-packages``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:25
msgid "The directives then work as in Sphinx:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:37
msgid "Every option Sphinx 9.1 accepts is accepted, with the same meaning: ``:members:``, ``:undoc-members:``, ``:private-members:``, ``:special-members:``, ``:inherited-members:``, ``:exclude-members:``, ``:member-order:``, ``:show-inheritance:``, ``:imported-members:``, ``:ignore-module-all:``, ``:class-doc-from:``, ``:no-value:``, ``:annotation:``, ``:no-index:``, ``:no-index-entry:``, ``:synopsis:``, ``:platform:``, ``:deprecated:``, and the ``no-`` forms that cancel ``autodoc_default_options``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:45
msgid "Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:48
msgid "These ``conf.toml`` settings have Sphinx's names, values, and defaults."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:50
msgid "``autodoc_source_paths``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:51
msgid "Guidedog's own: the folders Python modules are found in, in order, relative to the source folder. Only files below them are read, and a path through a symbolic link that leads elsewhere is refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:55
msgid "``autoclass_content``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:56
msgid "``\"class\"`` (default), ``\"init\"``, or ``\"both\"``: which docstrings describe a class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:58
msgid "``autodoc_class_signature``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:59
msgid "``\"mixed\"`` (default) or ``\"separated\"``, which documents ``__init__`` as a method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:61
msgid "``autodoc_default_options``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:62
msgid "A table of options every directive gets, such as ``members = true`` or ``member-order = \"bysource\"``. A value of ``false`` leaves the option out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:65
msgid "``autodoc_docstring_signature``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:66
msgid "``true`` (default): a first docstring line like ``name(args) -> result`` is the signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:69
msgid "``autodoc_inherit_docstrings``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:70
msgid "``true`` (default): a member without a docstring takes its base class's."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:72
msgid "``autodoc_member_order``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:73
msgid "``\"alphabetical\"`` (default), ``\"groupwise\"``, or ``\"bysource\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:75
msgid "``autodoc_preserve_defaults``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:76
msgid "``false`` (default): defaults are shown as ``repr()`` would write them. ``true`` shows them as written."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:79
msgid "``autodoc_typehints``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:80
msgid "``\"signature\"`` (default), ``\"description\"`` (as ``:type:`` and ``:rtype:`` fields), ``\"both\"``, or ``\"none\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:83
msgid "``autodoc_typehints_description_target``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:84
msgid "``\"all\"`` (default), ``\"documented\"``, or ``\"documented_params\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:86
msgid "``autodoc_typehints_format``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:87
msgid "``\"short\"`` (default) or ``\"fully-qualified\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:89
msgid "``autodoc_use_type_comments``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:90
msgid "``true`` (default): ``# type:`` comments count as annotations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:92
msgid "``strip_signature_backslash``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:93
msgid "Doubles backslashes in signatures, as in Sphinx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:95
msgid "``autodoc_mock_imports``, ``autodoc_type_aliases``, ``autodoc_warningiserror``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:96
msgid "Accepted. Nothing is imported, so nothing needs mocking. Type aliases are not applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:99
msgid "How Guidedog knows what Python would do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:102
msgid "Names are followed as Python binds them. ``from .app import Flask`` in a package's ``__init__.py`` makes ``flask.Flask`` the class defined in ``flask/app.py``. Autodoc then shows it with ``:canonical: flask.app.Flask``. Base classes are resolved the same way. Their method resolution order is computed as Python computes it, and inherited members come from the bases' source. Names imported only under ``if TYPE_CHECKING:`` do not exist when the module runs, so annotations naming them stay as written, as they do in Sphinx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:110
msgid "Docstrings are the strings Python would store: escapes processed and indentation removed as Python 3.13's compiler removes it. Attribute documentation comes from the places Sphinx's analyser reads: a ``#:`` comment after the assignment or on the lines above it, and a string literal after it. A function's signature comes from its ``def``. A class's signature comes from its metaclass ``__call__``, ``__new__``, or ``__init__``, as Python finds it. A dataclass's or ``NamedTuple``'s signature comes from its fields. ``@overload`` variants replace the implementation's signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:118
msgid "What cannot be known without running Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:121
msgid "Guidedog says so, naming the object, whenever the source cannot tell it what Python would. It gives a warning when something cannot be documented. It gives a note (shown with ``-v``) when it is documented with less than Sphinx would show."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:125
msgid "A compiled module (``.so``, ``.pyd``) has no source to read: its objects are not found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:127
msgid "A value made by running code (``app = Flask(__name__)``, ``now = datetime.now()``) has no ``:value:``, and a computed default is shown as written."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:129
msgid "A function made by a decorator from outside the source paths (``@click.command()``) is documented as the function it decorates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:131
msgid "A class from a package outside the source paths is named by the path it was imported from, which may differ from the module that defines it. Its members, its constructor's signature, and the docstrings it would pass on are unknown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:134
msgid "Docstrings of builtin types are unknown, so a method that has none and would inherit one from ``dict`` is left out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:136
msgid "Python extensions that handle autodoc's events (``autodoc-process-docstring``, ``autodoc-skip-member``, ...) do not run. napoleon is built in, below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:138
msgid "A module nested far deeper than real code (Python itself refuses 200 nested brackets; Guidedog also refuses a ``+`` chain of a few hundred operands) is not analysed, with the warning ``autodoc.too_deep``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:141
msgid "Work that multiplies is bounded. A name followed through imports and aliases stops after 48 steps along one path and 100,000 in all. A type alias or constant is expanded for 10,000 steps and is otherwise shown as written. A class's MRO is followed 100 classes deep. One directive generates at most 10,000 directives (the warning ``autodoc.limit``); only a class that contains itself under its own name reaches this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:147
msgid "Adding the missing packages' source folders to ``autodoc_source_paths`` closes most of these."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:150
msgid "Google and NumPy docstrings (napoleon)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:153
msgid "Many projects write docstrings in the Google or the NumPy style. We list ``sphinx.ext.napoleon``, and autodoc reads both styles:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:159
msgid "This docstring:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:174
msgid "reads as if it were written with fields:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:187
msgid "The conversion is the one Sphinx's napoleon makes, section for section: parameters, keyword arguments, returns, yields, raises, attributes, methods, examples, notes, references, \"See Also\", and the admonitions (``Note:``, ``Warning:``, ...). NumPy sections run first, then Google sections, as in Sphinx. Guidedog compares its conversion with every conversion of Sphinx's own napoleon tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:189
msgid "All sixteen settings keep their names and defaults: ``napoleon_google_docstring`` and ``napoleon_numpy_docstring`` (true), ``napoleon_include_init_with_doc``, ``napoleon_include_private_with_doc`` and ``napoleon_include_special_with_doc`` (false; they document ``__init__``, ``_private`` and ``__special__`` members that have docstrings), ``napoleon_use_admonition_for_examples``, ``napoleon_use_admonition_for_notes``, ``napoleon_use_admonition_for_references`` and ``napoleon_use_ivar`` (false), ``napoleon_use_param``, ``napoleon_use_rtype`` and ``napoleon_use_keyword`` (true), ``napoleon_preprocess_types`` (false: link the names in NumPy type specifications), ``napoleon_type_aliases`` (a table), ``napoleon_custom_sections`` and ``napoleon_attr_annotations`` (true: an attribute without a type takes its annotation). A custom section is a name, or a pair of a name and the section it behaves like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:195
msgid "Where napoleon cannot read a docstring, Guidedog says so. ``napoleon.section`` reports a Google header whose body is not indented below it, ``napoleon.field`` a parameter line without a name it can tell apart (``x the value`` instead of ``x: the value``), and ``napoleon.type`` an unclosed brace or quote in a type that ``napoleon_preprocess_types`` converts. The warning names the docstring and its line. From that line on the docstring is kept as written, so nothing is garbled silently. ``suppress_warnings = [\"napoleon\"]`` silences these warnings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:197
msgid "Summary tables and stub pages (autosummary)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:200
msgid "``sphinx.ext.autosummary`` makes a table of objects: each name with its shortened signature and the first sentence of its docstring."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:210
msgid "With ``:toctree:`` each entry gets a stub page in the folder ``generated`` beside the document. The stub of a module lists its attributes, functions, classes and exceptions in tables of their own. With ``:recursive:`` it also lists the package's submodules, and each of them gets a stub page in turn. The stub pages are listed in a hidden table of contents, so they appear in the navigation. The ``autolink`` role refers to a Python object as ``:py:obj:`` does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:212
msgid "Sphinx writes stub pages as ``.rst`` files into the source folder. Guidedog makes them in memory, as generated documents, and reads them like any other page. The source folder stays clean, and a stub changes as soon as its source does. A page we wrote ourselves at a stub's name wins over the stub, with the note ``autosummary.exists``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:214
msgid "The options are Sphinx's: ``:toctree:``, ``:caption:``, ``:template:``, ``:recursive:``, ``:signatures:`` (``long``, ``short`` or ``none``), ``:nosignatures:`` and ``:class:``. The stubs come from Sphinx's templates ``autosummary/base.rst``, ``module.rst`` and ``class.rst``, rendered with the variables Sphinx gives them (``fullname``, ``name``, ``objtype``, ``members``, ``functions``, ``classes``, ``exceptions``, ``attributes``, ``methods``, ``modules``, ...). A file of the same name under ``templates_path`` replaces a template; ``{% extends \"!autosummary/class.rst\" %}`` extends the built-in one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:216
msgid "The settings are ``autosummary_generate`` (true; false makes no stubs, a list makes the stubs of those files only), ``autosummary_context`` (more template variables), ``autosummary_filename_map`` (a stub's page name by object name), ``autosummary_imported_members`` (false) and ``autosummary_ignore_module_all`` (true). ``autosummary_mock_imports`` is accepted and does nothing, since nothing is imported; ``autosummary_generate_overwrite`` likewise, since no file is ever written."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:218
msgid "``autosummary.import`` names an entry the source scan cannot find, with the folders it searched. ``autosummary.template`` reports a template that fails, ``autosummary.toctree`` a ``:toctree:`` folder outside the source folder, and ``autosummary.limit`` a recursive walk cut short: a build makes at most 10,000 stub pages, nested at most 64 deep."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:220
msgid "Pages for whole packages (apidoc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:223
msgid "``sphinx.ext.apidoc`` writes a page for every package of a folder, as ``sphinx-apidoc`` does. Each entry of ``apidoc_modules`` names the package folder (relative to ``conf.toml``) and the folder of its pages (relative to the source folder):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:234
msgid "The pages are ``api/shop`` for the package, ``api/shop.cart`` for each module with ``separate_modules``, and ``api/modules``, the table of contents a ``toctree`` can name. Their text is the text ``sphinx-apidoc`` writes, with its ``automodule`` directives. Like stub pages, they are generated documents and never files. The package folder joins the folders autodoc reads, so no ``autodoc_source_paths`` entry is needed for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:236
msgid "An entry may set ``exclude_patterns``, ``max_depth``, ``follow_links``, ``separate_modules``, ``include_private``, ``no_headings``, ``module_first``, ``implicit_namespaces`` and ``automodule_options``; the settings ``apidoc_exclude_patterns``, ``apidoc_max_depth`` (4), and so on, give the rest. As in ``sphinx-apidoc``, ``automodule_options`` applies to module pages, and a package page uses ``members``, ``show-inheritance`` and ``undoc-members``. ``apidoc.path`` reports a package folder that does not exist, ``apidoc.exists`` a page we wrote ourselves that wins over a generated one, and ``apidoc.limit`` a walk of more than 10,000 modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:238
msgid "What is not documented (coverage)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:241
msgid "``sphinx.ext.coverage`` adds a builder. ``guidedog build coverage`` reads the documents and compares the Python objects they describe with the objects the source defines:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:252
msgid "``_build/coverage/python.txt`` lists, per module, the public functions, classes and methods no document describes, then the statistics, in Sphinx's layout. The modules checked are ``coverage_modules`` with their submodules, or, when it is empty, the modules the documents describe. ``coverage_ignore_modules``, ``coverage_ignore_functions`` and ``coverage_ignore_classes`` are patterns that match at the start of a name, ``coverage_ignore_pyobjects`` patterns that match anywhere in a full name. ``coverage_write_headline``, ``coverage_statistics_to_report``, ``coverage_statistics_to_stdout``, ``coverage_skip_undoc_in_source`` and ``coverage_show_missing_items`` (each missing object as the note ``coverage.undocumented``) work as in Sphinx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:254
msgid "C headers are checked when ``coverage_c_path`` names them (relative to the source folder) and ``coverage_c_regexes`` gives, per object type, a pattern whose first group is an item's name. The items no ``c:`` description names go to ``c.txt``; ``coverage_ignore_c_items`` leaves some out. ``coverage.module`` reports a module that is not in the source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:256
msgid "Rebuilding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:259
msgid "Every Python file an autodoc directive read is a dependency of the page. When we edit it, the next build reads the page again. Stub pages and apidoc pages are made again on every build, from the source as it is then. A page whose object was not found is read again on every build, so it picks the object up as soon as the source has it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:263
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../autodoc.rst:266
msgid "Building documentation never runs the project's code. Guidedog reads Python source as text, with the same confinement it applies to includes: only below ``autodoc_source_paths``, and not through links leading elsewhere."
msgstr ""
